WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.429 --> 00:00:04.390
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.390 --> 00:00:09.240
Through over 200 episodes of this series,
we talked about many learning-based algorithms

00:00:09.240 --> 00:00:14.010
that are able to solve problems that previously
seemed completely impossible.

00:00:14.010 --> 00:00:19.820
They can look at an image and describe what
they depict in a sentence, or even turn video

00:00:19.820 --> 00:00:22.930
game graphics into reality and back.

00:00:22.930 --> 00:00:26.380
Amazing new results keep appearing every single
week.

00:00:26.380 --> 00:00:30.949
However, an important thing that we need to
solve is that if we deploy these neural networks

00:00:30.949 --> 00:00:35.520
in a production environment, we would want
to know if we're relying on a good or bad

00:00:35.520 --> 00:00:36.910
AI's decision.

00:00:36.910 --> 00:00:41.559
The narrative is very simple: if we don't
trust a classifier, we won't use it.

00:00:41.559 --> 00:00:46.510
And perhaps the best way of earning the trust
of a human would be if the AI could explain

00:00:46.510 --> 00:00:48.360
how it came to a given decision.

00:00:48.360 --> 00:00:53.140
Strictly speaking, a neural network can explain
it to us, but it would show us hundreds of

00:00:53.140 --> 00:00:59.150
thousands of neuron activations that are completely
unusable for any sort of intuitive reasoning.

00:00:59.150 --> 00:01:04.570
So, what is even more difficult to solve is
that this explanation happens in a way that

00:01:04.570 --> 00:01:06.030
we can interpret.

00:01:06.030 --> 00:01:10.610
An earlier approach used decision trees that
described what the learner looks at and how

00:01:10.610 --> 00:01:14.060
it uses this information to arrive to a conclusion.

00:01:14.060 --> 00:01:15.520
This new work is quite different.

00:01:15.520 --> 00:01:20.159
For instance, imagine that a neural network
would look at all the information we know

00:01:20.159 --> 00:01:24.130
about a patient and tell us that this patient
likely has the flu.

00:01:24.130 --> 00:01:28.689
And, in the meantime, it could tell us that
the fact that the patient has a headache and

00:01:28.689 --> 00:01:33.740
sneezes a lot contributed to the conclusion
that he has the flu, but, the lack of fatigue

00:01:33.740 --> 00:01:35.520
is notable evidence against it.

00:01:35.520 --> 00:01:39.950
Our doctor could take this information and
instead of blindly relying on the output,

00:01:39.950 --> 00:01:42.430
could make a more informed decision.

00:01:42.430 --> 00:01:47.770
A fine example of a case where AI does not
replace, but augment human labor.

00:01:47.770 --> 00:01:50.370
An elegant tool for a more civilized age.

00:01:50.370 --> 00:01:55.869
Here, we see an example image where the classifier
explains which region contributes to the decision

00:01:55.869 --> 00:02:00.610
that this image depicts a cat, and which region
seems to be counterevidence.

00:02:00.610 --> 00:02:06.049
We can use this not only for tabulated patient
data and images, but text as well.

00:02:06.049 --> 00:02:11.689
In this other example, we try to find out
whether a piece of written text is about Christinanity

00:02:11.689 --> 00:02:13.409
or Atheism.

00:02:13.409 --> 00:02:18.170
Note that the decision itself is not as simple
as looking for a few keywords, even a mid-tier

00:02:18.170 --> 00:02:21.340
classifier is much more sophisticated than
that.

00:02:21.340 --> 00:02:24.370
But, it can tell us about the main contributing
factors.

00:02:24.370 --> 00:02:29.599
A big additional selling point is that this
technique is model agnostic, which means that

00:02:29.599 --> 00:02:34.790
it can be applied to other learning algorithms
that are able to perform classification.

00:02:34.790 --> 00:02:40.049
It is also a possibility that an AI is only
right by chance, and if this is the case,

00:02:40.049 --> 00:02:41.919
we should definitely know about that.

00:02:41.919 --> 00:02:46.829
And here, in this example, with the additional
explanation, it is rather easy to find out

00:02:46.829 --> 00:02:51.409
that we have a bad model that looks at the
background of the image and thinks it is the

00:02:51.409 --> 00:02:53.019
fur of a wolf.

00:02:53.019 --> 00:02:58.629
The tests indicate that humans make significantly
better decisions when they lean on explanations

00:02:58.629 --> 00:03:00.719
that are extracted by this technique.

00:03:00.719 --> 00:03:03.480
The source code of this project is also available.

00:03:03.480 --> 00:03:07.520
Thanks for watching and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

